WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
Good morning, John!

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:06.250
In my last video, I said that the order of
the alphabet is older that any word in our

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:07.250
language.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:11.120
And that's kind of true; no word is spelled
or pronounced the way it was back then, but

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:17.030
there are some words that stretch back a really
long time, like before written language—

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:18.450
before agriculture, even.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:24.100
We can tell because they show up in languages
that haven't been related for a really long

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.100
time.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.450
And one of those hyper-conserved words that
appears in Norse and Slavik and Sanskrit and

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:30.460
Greek is the word "fart."

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:35.960
Fart is one of the oldest words in the English
language, and that is just one of the 14 facts

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.510
that I'm gonna tell you about farts today!

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:38.510
Why?

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.530
Because I'm the master of my own destiny,
and I get to do what I want.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:43.890
Fart Fact #2
Benjamin Franklin's Fart Proudly

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:47.750
Benjamin Franklin wrote a letter to the Royal
Society called "Fart Proudly."

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.710
In the letter, he calls on them to create
a kind of X Prize, to give a reward to a scientist

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:56.690
who can make farts smell not just non-smelly,
but good.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.950
Fart Fact #3
It Could Be Worse!

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.150
More than 99% of the gas in your farts is
actually odorless.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:06.470
Farts are mainly composed of carbon dioxide,
hydrogen, sometimes nitrogen and oxygen and

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:07.470
methane.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.120
Fart Fact #4
Sometimes Methane?!

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.110
Yes, only sometimes methane!

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.530
About half of people don't have methane in
their farts!

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.760
Whether or not you do depends on the kind
of organisms that live in your intestines...

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:18.760
it's weird.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.750
Fart Fact #5
...But Odorless?

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.220
Methane has no smell!

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.200
Methane is also called natural gas; we use
it a lot in our houses.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.970
Now, before it's sent to our houses, an odor
is added to it.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:32.890
If there's a leak, you want to be able to
smell it before it explodes and everything

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:33.890
dies.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:37.290
The chemical most commonly used for this is
tert-Butylthiol, which is a sulfur-containing

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:38.290
compound.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:39.970
Sulfur compounds are notoriously stinky.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:42.130
Fart Fact #6
The Skunk Junk In Your Trunk

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:45.000
And yes, sulfur compounds are the things that
make farts stinky.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.189
So that one percent contains stuff like hydrogen
sulfide, which is TERRIBLY SMELLY because

00:01:50.189 --> 00:01:52.360
it's very toxic and we want to know if it's
around.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:53.840
Fart Fact #7
Franklin's Dream Realized!

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.909
There are now actually medicines that you
can take that bind to sulfur compounds so

00:01:57.909 --> 00:01:59.950
your farts don't smell as bad.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.750
Fart Fact #8
Bovine Butt Burps

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:04.509
Now, people don't always fart methane, but
cows, they do, right?

00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:09.009
NO, I mean, maybe a little bit, but cows DO
contribute a massive amount of methane to

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:12.000
the atmosphere where it is a potent greenhouse
gas, which is a problem.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.520
But the vast majority of that gas is not released
from the back end, but from the front.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:17.520
In burps.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.450
Fart Fact #9
Cinematic Toots

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:25.310
It wasn't until 1974's Blazing Saddles that
the first audible fart was heard in a movie

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:26.310
theater.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.490
Well, in a movie.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:30.510
Probably there were people that farted in
movie theaters.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.310
Fart Fact #10
Literary Egg Laying

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:36.280
The first written fart joke though: ih—
bvwoo-eh-uhh.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.489
James Joyce, Shakespeare, Dante, Chaucer,
Arabian Nights: all contain fart jokes.

00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:42.830
Fart Fact #11
The Prime Hiney Whine

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:47.069
The earliest joke in history (that we have
a record of) is a fart joke.

00:02:47.069 --> 00:02:50.220
I'm taking some liberties here as a translator,
but basically, here's the joke:

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.250
Only one thing of all things has not yet happened:
the young lady ne'er farted in her new husband's

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:56.250
lap.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:00.110
In the 4,000 years since that joke was written,
it's -- ah, my guess is that's happened.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:02.370
Fart Fart #12
The Fartomaniac

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:06.540
Some people are able to actually take air
into their rectum intentionally.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.970
And that is the trick behind Le Pétomane,
who was a 19th century performer who was able

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:14.390
to play the melodies of popular songs with
his butt.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:19.410
He performed for princes and kings and for
Sigmund Freud, but he was not the first flatulist.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:23.630
Augustine recorded being entertained by one
such person in the 5th century.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:25.660
Fart Fact #13
Farty Tarty!

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:26.660
Excuse me a moment.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:27.660
I must prepare.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.470
(blows loud raspberry into his arm)
Call that blowin' a raspberry.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:30.470
Why?

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:33.000
Because in British rhyming slang, "farts"
were "raspberry tarts."

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.050
Fart Fact #14
A Mighty Wind

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:39.800
The average person farts over ten times per
day, about a liter of total gas in volume.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:44.900
Since around 25% of that gas is hydrogen,
it would take two million people's farts over

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:50.060
a full year to fill up the Hindenburg with
hydrogen.I DID THE MATH MYSELF.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:51.810
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

